From: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 12:39:38 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] net: bgmac: offload flows to the Northstar flow accelerator

With the flow accelerator in the path (previous patch), let it forward
the connections that a netfilter flowtable offloads. DSA passes the
flowtable block from the user ports to the conduit's ndo_setup_tc, so
bgmac gets one, and NETIF_F_HW_TC to switch it off.

Offloaded: IPv4 TCP and UDP between two user ports of the switch behind
this conduit, routed or with NAT of the source or of the destination
(address and port). Everything else stays in software: IPv6, VLAN and
PPPoE, both kinds of NAT at once, fragments.

Each direction is one entry of the 1024-entry flow table, in one of the
four slots selected by the software hash (CRC-ITU-T over the tuple, as
fa_crc_ccitt() in Broadcom's etc_fa.c). The entry points to a next hop
(destination MAC) and a pool entry (source MAC), shared between flows.
The table formats and the access sequence follow etc_fa.c; the
flow_rule parsing follows mtk_ppe_offload.c.

Three details found on the hardware:
- The FA hands its output to the switch with a tag that leaves the
  forwarding to the switch's address table. DSA keeps learning off on
  standalone ports such as the WAN port, so the switch flooded every
  offloaded frame to all ports. Give it a static FDB entry for a next
  hop behind a standalone port, as long as the next hop is in use.
- The flowtable offloads the two directions separately and retries only
  when both were refused. A direction whose reply does not fit in its
  bucket is refused too, so the connection stays in software as a whole
  and is retried later.
- nf_tables unbinds a flowtable block on a ruleset change and tears its
  connections down only after the last driver unbound, so the driver
  never hears about them; a firewall reload left the old entries
  forwarding outside the new ruleset. Remove a block's flows when it is
  unbound.

The tables are initialised on every open, so stop drops all flows; the
flowtable then offloads the connections again from software.

The FA counts hits per entry but not bytes: the flowtable gets packet
counts and the last-used time.

Known limits, for the patches that follow: the FA drops IPv4 packets
with TTL 1 silently instead of passing them to Linux for the ICMP time
exceeded, and it forwards a TCP FIN or RST without telling the
flowtable, so the connection stays established in conntrack until it
times out.

Measured on a Linksys EA6900 (BCM4708), iperf3 TCP between a LAN port
and the WAN port through NAT, upload/download in Mbit/s:
- no offloading:              367/438, one CPU core busy
- software flow offloading:   456/535, one CPU core busy
- hardware flow offloading:   941/941, 1-3 % CPU
- port forward from the WAN:  949/926, 1 % CPU
Thirty firewall reloads during one transfer kept it at 938 Mbit/s with
exactly two entries in the table each time, and the table was empty
again a minute after the transfer.

After a reload, conntrack sometimes shows such a connection as
[OFFLOAD] instead of [HW_OFFLOAD] while the hardware forwards it: the
old and the new flowtable share the conntrack entry, and the old one's
delete work can clear IPS_HW_OFFLOAD after the new one's add work set
it. That is the flowtable core, not this driver.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
---
